eed the animals  we're really struggling to grow enough  crops to make crops for winter for the  animals to eat  and there's nothing now for the animals  when they're needing extra for the  autumn time there's nothing for the  lambs when we take them off the mothers  the rest of july and all august two of  the drives won't steal the crumbs so  it's not as if a shelter rain's going to  put things right now it's a long time  before we're going to get the autumn  rains that will probably correct things  would be then you know it might be too  late for feeding the animals for winter  this is a fifth generation family farm  and keith stones who you heard from in  that clip says that he cannot remember a  prolonged dry spell like this since the  1970s and really whether or not this  prolonged dry spell turns into an  official drought is somewhat  insignificant for patches of ground that  already look like this the grass is  beyond saving it is already dead but  what will be key is whether there is  enough rain in the next few weeks to  enable new planting for new grass to be  able to germinate and then grow because  that is what will go on to feed  livestock over winter and for many farms  will be the difference between making  some sort of profit this year and not  okay let's get a bit more of the day's  climate news now and in greece  firefighters have contained a wildfire  on the island of lesbos after three days  while a heatwave continues across much  of the south of the country fire  warnings remain in place and national  parks have been closed to the public at  the deer national park in the northeast  of the country authorities are trying to  contain a wildfire that's been burning  for five days  now the world's first ever heat event to  be named has been detected in spain's  fourth largest city seville just a month  after the naming scheme was launched now  authorities have warned that heatwave  zoe could bring temperatures of up to 43  degrees celsius today and t